"""The field pages (/categories/<field>/): what each one answers besides the list of jobs.

Owner, 19-25 Nov 2026: every field must be its own page, made for the search people type ("sales jobs", "healthcare jobs"), better than
the first results and the AI answers for that search. The search check of 25 Nov 2026 (ChatGPT in a temporary chat and Bing, 25 queries,
record in SERP_AI_CHECK.md) found that the answers cover: the kinds of work in the field, the titles open to beginners, pay, whether
remote jobs are open outside the US, skills and licences, how to start, and where to look. So each field has here:

- `key`: the words of the search, first in the title ("Sales Jobs (94,684 open, ...)"); `h1` the heading;
- `cats`: the job-list categories (/jobs/<cat>/) that are the same field: those pages name this one as canonical, so the two never
  compete for one search (build.py, one_page_per_query);
- `ooh`: the US Bureau of Labor Statistics occupation groups of the field (Occupational Outlook Handbook, read 25 Nov 2026: projections
  2025-35, median wages of May 2025), and `occ`, its occupations shown with the schooling the BLS names for a first job;
- `ask`: what the field's postings ask for, counted in the build from their own words: (label for the row of figures, regex);
- `steps`: how to get a job in the field, written for this field only;
- `faq`: questions the AI answers and searches raise for the field, answered from the BLS pages named above or plain rules of the
  trade; figures that change are counted in the build, never typed here.
"""

